ation. A plan can set a lower deferral cap than the legal limit, and the plan decides whether to offer catch-up contributions. Tools for this journey 401(k) calculator → 2027 Contribution Limits at a Glance The 2027 401(k) contribution limit is $25,500 for employee deferrals, up $1,000 from 2026. The same deferral figure applies to 403(b) plans, governmental 457 plans and the TSP. The table separates the regular limit from catch-ups so you can use the row that matches your age and account. Limit 2026 2027 Change Employee deferral: 401(k), 403(b), governmental 457 and TSP $24,500 $25,500 Up $1,000 Catch-up, generally age 50 or older $8,000 $8,500 Up $500 Deferral plus catch-up, generally age 50 or older $32,500 $34,000 Up $1,500 Higher catch-up, ages 60 to 63 $11,250 $11,750 Up $500 Deferral plus higher catch-up, ages 60 to 63, by our calculation $35,750 $37,250 Up $1,500 IRA contribution $7,500 $7,500 Unchanged IRA catch-up, age 50 or older $1,100 $1,100 Unchanged General SIMPLE plan contribution $17,000 $17,500 Up $500 Defined contribution plan limit under section 415(c) $72,000 $75,000 Up $3,000 Roth catch-up wage threshold used for the following year's designation $150,000 $155,000 Up $5,000 The $37,250 figure adds the IRS's $25,500 deferral limit and $11,750 higher catch-up. The IRS publishes the catch-up amount but does not give that combined total in its announcement. For the prior year's employee deferral and catch-up figures, use the 2026 401(k) limits guide . The Roth catch-up threshold row needs a different reading from the contribution rows. Notice 2026-66 describes the $155,000 figure as the 2026 threshold used to determine the designation of certain 2027 catch-up contributions. It is not an amount you can contribute. 401(k), 403(b), 457 and TSP Deferral Limits for 2027 Employees can defer up to $25,500 in 2027 under the listed 401(k), 403(b), governmental 457 and TSP limits. The 2026 figure was $24,500. That $1,000 increase changes the annual legal ceiling, but an individual plan may cap deferrals below it. For participants age 50 or older, the general catch-up rises from $8,000 to $8,500. Adding $8,500 to $25,500 produces the $34,000 total stated by the IRS. The corresponding 2026 total was $32,500, so the combined amount rises $1,500. Ask the plan administrator whether catch-up contributions are available before setting a payroll election that assumes the full amount. Employees ages 60, 61, 62 and 63 have a higher $11,750 catch-up instead of the $8,500 general catch-up. It was $11,250 in 2026. By our calculation, $25,500 plus $11,750 equals $37,250 for 2027. The same addition gives $35,750 for 2026, a $1,500 difference between the two totals. The separate $75,000 defined contribution plan limit under section 415(c) rises from $72,000 in 2026. The 2026 401(k) limits guide describes that limit as covering employee deferrals and employer contributions, including a match or profit sharing. Do not use $75,000 as an employee-only payroll deferral target; the employee deferral figure in the first row of the table is $25,500 before an applicable catch-up. Roth and Traditional 401(k) employee contributions share one deferral limit. Splitting an election between the two does not create a second $25,500 allowance. Federal employees comparing plan features can use the TSP vs 401(k) comparison ; the IRS lists both under the same 2027 employee deferral figure. Roth Catch-Up Wage Threshold for 2027 The $155,000 Roth catch-up wage threshold is used to determine whether certain 2027 catch-up contributions must be designated Roth. IRS Notice 2026-66 states: > The Roth catch-up wage threshold for 2026, which under section 414(v)(7)(A) is used to determine whether an individual's catch-up contributions to an applicable employer plan (other than a plan described in section 408(k) or (p)) for 2027 must be designated as Roth contributions, is increased from $150,000 to $155,000. This threshold applies to catch-up contributions only. The notice excludes plans described in section 408(k) or (p), including simplified employee pension and SIMPLE plans. Whether an employer plan offers Roth contributions is plan-specific. Ask the plan administrator what catch-up elections the plan permits before choosing a designation. Keep the threshold separate from the contribution amount. The $25,500 figure is the regular 2027 employee deferral limit; $155,000 is the threshold identified in the notice for the catch-up designation. The notice's wording is important when discussing timing: it identifies a threshold for 2026 that is used for 2027 catch-up contributions. IRA Limits and Income Phase-Outs for 2027 The 2027 IRA contribution limit stays at $7,500, and the age-50 catch-up stays at $1,100. Together, those two amounts equal $8,600 for someone eligible for the catch-up. Unlike the unchanged contribution amounts, several income phase-out ranges rise from 2026 to 2027. Income phase-out range 2026 2027 Traditional IRA deduction: single taxpayer covered by a workplace plan $81,000 to $91,000 $83,000 to $93,000 Traditional IRA deduction: married filing jointly, contributing spouse covered $129,000 to $149,000 $133,000 to $153,000 Traditional IRA deduction: contributor not covered, spouse covered $242,000 to $252,000 $250,000 to $260,000 Traditional IRA deduction: married filing separately and covered $0 to $10,000 $0 to $10,000 Roth IRA contribution: single or head of household $153,000 to $168,000 $158,000 to $173,000 Roth IRA contribution: married filing jointly $242,000 to $252,000 $250,000 to $260,000 Roth IRA contribution: married filing separately $0 to $10,000 $0 to $10,000 The Traditional IRA rows describe deduction phase-outs under the workplace-coverage conditions named in each row. If neither spouse is covered by a retirement plan at work, those deduction phase-outs do not apply. The Roth IRA rows describe contribution income phase-outs, so do not treat a Traditional IRA deduction range as the Roth contribution range for the same filing status. The 2026 IRA limits guide provides the prior-year figures for a separate election or tax-year comparison. Notice 2026-66 lists Saver's Credit income figures of $83,000 for married couples filing jointly, $62,250 for heads of household and $41,500 for singles and married taxpayers filing separately. The corresponding 2026 figures were $80,500, $60,375 and $40,250. The notice says the 2027 figures apply to contributions to ABLE accounts made after December 31, 2026, because Saver's Match replaces the Saver's Credit for elective contributions to qualifying retirement plans and IRAs. Do not use the listed Saver's Credit figures alone to decide how a retirement-plan contribution will be treated. SIMPLE Plan Limits for 2027 The general SIMPLE retirement account contribution limit rises to $17,500 in 2027 from $17,000 in 2026. Certain applicable SIMPLE accounts have a higher $19,100 limit, up from $18,100. Those are different base amounts, so identify which SIMPLE limit the plan uses before turning an annual target into a payroll election. The general SIMPLE catch-up for someone age 50 or older remains $4,000. A different $3,850 catch-up applies to certain applicable SIMPLE plans and is also unchanged. For ages 60 to 63, the higher SIMPLE catch-up remains $5,250. These SIMPLE catch-up amounts differ from the $8,500 general catch-up and $11,750 higher catch-up listed for the 401(k), 403(b), governmental 457 and TSP group. The Roth catch-up wage-threshold sentence in Notice 2026-66 excludes SIMPLE plans. Keep that exclusion separate from the SIMPLE contribution amounts: the SIMPLE limits above still describe how much may be contributed under their stated categories. Ask the plan administrator which base limit and catch-up category apply to the plan before setting an election. What Maxing Out 2027 Takes Per Paycheck A $25,500 annual deferral takes $980.77 from each of 26 equal biweekly paychecks, by our calculation. At ModernWallet, 