ith an unchanged $1,100 catch-up. Those legal figures may be higher than an employer plan permits. A plan can cap deferrals below the legal limit and decides whether to offer catch-up contributions. Roth availability is also plan-specific, and Notice 2026-66 sets out a threshold used for certain 2027 catch-up designations. Request the plan's election details from its administrator if any of those conditions could affect the amount entered in payroll. Someone using a different plan type, including a self-employed saver whose plan is outside the listed deferral categories, should use that plan's document rather than the 401(k) row. For a covered plan, our per-paycheck calculation also changes when a biweekly calendar has 27 checks instead of 26. Compare the plan's permitted amount and the year's pay dates with the 2027 401(k) contribution limit, then update your payroll election. Frequently asked questions What is the 401(k) contribution limit for 2027? The 2027 employee deferral limit is $25,500. An eligible participant age 50 or older can generally add an $8,500 catch-up, for $34,000 total. At ages 60 to 63, the $11,750 higher catch-up gives a $37,250 total by our calculation, if the plan permits catch-up contributions. Is the 2027 401(k) limit final or expected? The $25,500 figure is an announced 2027 limit, not an estimate. The IRS announced it on November 12, 2026. An individual plan may still set a lower employee deferral cap. What is the 2027 Roth 401(k) contribution limit? Roth and Traditional 401(k) employee contributions share the $25,500 deferral limit; they do not each receive a separate limit. An eligible catch-up may increase the total by age. Notice 2026-66 also states a $155,000 threshold used to determine whether certain 2027 catch-up contributions must be designated Roth. Is the TSP limit the same as the 401(k) limit for 2027? Yes. The IRS lists the TSP with 401(k), 403(b) and governmental 457 plans at a $25,500 employee deferral limit for 2027. The 